= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Schmidt decomposition theorem> says that a normalized vector in the <tensor product> of two finite-dimensional <Hilbert spaces> can be written
$$
|\psi\rangle=\sum_{j=1}^r\sqrt{\lambda_j}\,|u_j\rangle\otimes|v_j\rangle,\qquad
\lambda_j>0,\quad\sum_{j=1}^r\lambda_j=1,\quad r\leq n_1,
$$
where both families are orthonormal. The numbers $\sqrt{\lambda_j}$ are its <Schmidt coefficients>. The <Schmidt rank> $r$ and coefficients, including multiplicities, are uniquely determined by the state; the local vectors may be changed within degenerate eigenspaces and by compensating phases. Zero coefficients may be appended to make $n_1$ terms, since $n_2\geq n_1$.

For a proof, form the <reduced density matrix> $\rho_1=\operatorname{Tr}_2|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|$. It is a <Hermitian matrix> that is <positive semidefinite>, with <trace> one. The <spectral theorem> supplies an <orthonormal basis> $u_1,\ldots,u_{n_1}$ of <eigenvectors> with nonnegative <eigenvalues> $\lambda_j$. Define the unnormalized relative vectors in the second <Hilbert space> by
$$
|w_j\rangle=(\langle u_j|\otimes I)|\psi\rangle.
$$
Completeness of the first <orthonormal basis> gives $|\psi\rangle=\sum_j|u_j\rangle|w_j\rangle$. In any <orthonormal basis> $e_b$ of the second factor, the definition of the <partial trace> gives
$$
\langle w_i|w_j\rangle
=\sum_b\langle\psi|u_i,e_b\rangle\langle u_j,e_b|\psi\rangle
=\langle u_j|\rho_1|u_i\rangle
=\lambda_i\delta_{ij}.
$$
Thus a zero <eigenvalue> gives $w_j=0$, and for each positive <eigenvalue> the vector $v_j=w_j/\sqrt{\lambda_j}$ has unit norm. Distinct such vectors are orthogonal by the same identity. Substituting them gives the required <Schmidt decomposition>, with at most $n_1$ nonzero terms.

Taking the other <partial trace> now gives
$$
\rho_1=\sum_{j=1}^r\lambda_j|u_j\rangle\langle u_j|,\qquad
\rho_2=\sum_{j=1}^r\lambda_j|v_j\rangle\langle v_j|.
$$
Therefore **the two reduced density matrices have identical nonzero eigenvalues, namely the squared Schmidt coefficients**. This also proves uniqueness of the coefficient multiset and shows that the <pure state> is a <product state> exactly when $r=1$.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $Q$ be Alice's unknown <qubit>, with <pure state> $|\phi\rangle_Q=\alpha|0\rangle+\beta|1\rangle$. Alice and Bob initially share the <Bell state> $|\Phi^+\rangle_{AB}=(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$. Alice holds $Q,A$ and Bob holds $B$. Define the <Bell basis>
$$
|\Phi^\pm\rangle=\frac{|00\rangle\pm|11\rangle}{\sqrt2},\qquad
|\Psi^\pm\rangle=\frac{|01\rangle\pm|10\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Expanding in this <orthonormal basis> gives the <Bell-basis teleportation identity>
$$
\begin{aligned}
|\phi\rangle_Q|\Phi^+\rangle_{AB}=\frac12\bigl(&|\Phi^+\rangle_{QA}|\phi\rangle_B
+|\Phi^-\rangle_{QA}Z|\phi\rangle_B\\
&+|\Psi^+\rangle_{QA}X|\phi\rangle_B
+|\Psi^-\rangle_{QA}XZ|\phi\rangle_B\bigr).
\end{aligned}
$$
For example, the last branch on $B$ is $\alpha|1\rangle-\beta|0\rangle$, which is $XZ|\phi\rangle$; the ordering of the <Pauli operators> therefore matters.

Alice performs a <projective measurement> in the <Bell basis> on her two local <qubits>. Each outcome has <probability> $1/4$, independent of the unknown amplitudes. She encodes the four possible outcomes as two classical bits and sends them to Bob. For outcomes $\Phi^+,\Phi^-,\Psi^+,\Psi^-$, respectively, Bob applies the <local unitary operations>
$$
I,\qquad Z,\qquad X,\qquad ZX.
$$
These are inverses of the corresponding branch <Pauli operators>, so **Bob obtains exactly the unknown input qubit in every branch**. Using $XZ$ instead of $ZX$ as the last correction also succeeds up to an irrelevant overall phase. Alice need not know $\alpha$ or $\beta$. Every quantum operation is local to one of the parties, and the only transmitted information is the two-bit classical outcome. The shared <Bell pair> is consumed.

Before the message arrives, Bob's <density operator> is
$$
\frac14\left(\rho+Z\rho Z+X\rho X+XZ\rho ZX\right)=\frac I2,
$$
so no unknown-state information is available without the <classical communication>. Alice's original <qubit> has been included in the destructive <Bell-basis measurement>; the protocol creates no extra copy. Since the corrected transformation is the identity on every input vector and is linear, it also preserves <entanglement> with an external reference and transfers mixed inputs. Thus this is deterministic <quantum teleportation>, using precisely one shared maximally entangled <Bell pair>, two classical bits, and local operations.

= 4
{parent=Paper 58}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <EPR criterion of reality> proposes that a quantity has an element of physical reality if its value can be predicted with certainty without disturbing the system. To use remote predictions this way, one additionally assumes locality: the choice and execution of a <quantum measurement> on separated particles cannot disturb the local physical properties of the remaining particle. The contradiction below is with this combination of the criterion and locality, rather than with a definition of reality alone.

Use the <computational basis> $|0\rangle=|\uparrow\rangle$, $|1\rangle=|\downarrow\rangle$ and the conventional <Pauli matrices>
$$
X|0\rangle=|1\rangle,\quad X|1\rangle=|0\rangle,\qquad
Y|0\rangle=i|1\rangle,\quad Y|1\rangle=-i|0\rangle.
$$
For each site $j$, let $O_j$ be the <tensor product> of $X$ at that site and $Y$ at all other sites. For $N=4M+3$, the number of $Y$ factors is $N-1=4M+2$, so
$$
O_j|0^N\rangle=i^{N-1}|1^N\rangle=-|1^N\rangle,\qquad
O_j|1^N\rangle=(-i)^{N-1}|0^N\rangle=-|0^N\rangle.
$$
The minus sign in the given <GHZ state> then implies
$$
O_j|\psi\rangle=|\psi\rangle\quad\text{for every }j,
\qquad X^{\otimes N}|\psi\rangle=-|\psi\rangle.
$$
Hence **every one-$X$, remaining-$Y$ product has measurement outcome $+1$ with certainty, while the all-$X$ product has outcome $-1$ with certainty**. These are experimentally separate local <Pauli measurements> on identically prepared copies, not joint measurements of $X$ and $Y$ on the same particle.

For a chosen site, the other $N-1$ sites can determine its $X$ outcome with certainty by measuring $Y$ and using the corresponding $O_j$ relation. They can determine its $Y$ outcome with certainty by selecting an $O_\ell$ with $\ell\ne j$, measuring $X$ at $\ell$ and $Y$ at all other remote sites. Since $N\geq3$, both choices are available. Locality and the <EPR criterion of reality> therefore assign each site definite values $x_j,y_j\in\{+1,-1\}$, independent of which remote choice is made, even though <quantum theory> does not jointly measure the local $X$ and $Y$.

The certain one-$X$ correlations require those assigned values to obey
$$
x_j\prod_{k\ne j}y_k=+1\qquad(j=1,\ldots,N).
$$
Multiplying these scalar equations gives
$$
1=\prod_{j=1}^N x_j\prod_{k=1}^N y_k^{N-1}=\prod_{j=1}^N x_j,
$$
since $N-1$ is even and $y_k^2=1$. Thus the all-$X$ product would necessarily be $+1$. But its quantum value is $-1$, giving
$$
\boxed{\prod_jx_j=+1\ \text{from local predetermined values},\qquad
\prod_jx_j=-1\ \text{from quantum theory}.}
$$
The equations cannot hold even for one assignment of values, so allowing a probability distribution over assignments cannot remove the contradiction. This is the <GHZ contradiction for N congruent to 3 modulo 4> for every $M\geq0$. Multiplying classical assigned values is crucial: one must not commute the incompatible local <Pauli operators> as though they were ordinary numbers. Their noncommutativity is exactly what permits the quantum correlations to evade the classical product argument.
